                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "i have a lot of respect for rpgmaker games, especially the horror ones that can be considered the \"classics,\" but this game is kind of a miserable experience, and i feel like theres definitely a reason less than 10% of players have the achievement for beating the game twice.\r\n\r\nthe gameplay somehow takes the worst parts of linear games, with almost no objects having unique interaction text besides major objectives (ex: pressing Z on a flower pot will say \"It's a flower pot.\" repeat this with 99% of objects in the game,) while also having the worst parts of open exploration games (giving you very little direction on where to go next, waiting for you to step on an invisible trigger that activates an event.)\r\n\r\nit gets even worse in what are supposed to be tense moments in the gameplay, such as chase scenes. in events where you're the one running from something, if you try to run in a direction the game doesnt want you to, it leads to an instant death. i don't think this would be a problem if you were given even the slightest idea on where you are supposed to run. even worse, during parts where you're the one chasing someone/something, the character will often run out of the room youre in, and give you no sense of where they ran to, often leading you to backtrack throughout the entire area, completely losing the tension of the moment as you check every room. this is especially bad in the last chapter, where your speed is nerfed for one of these sequences as you're supposed to chase a character upstairs. it doesn't need to be the iconic video game yellow paint, just give me sort of direction and let me run in it.\r\n\r\nthe puzzle part of the gameplay too is pretty monotonous too, with almost all of them boiling down to \"find a key/code for this object to get another key/code.\" at least some of them require some thought, but none of them really use the game or its engine in any creative ways, with most just being a string of numbers to type into something. it's not too much of an issue, but objective markers sometimes seem inconsistent. often theyre represented with a yellow sparkle, yet certain ones (like the box where you find the crowbar, screwdriver) are not. most of the time theyre still unique sprites which you'd investigate anyways, but with the previously mentioned point about objects not having unique interaction text, theres a chance you would just never find some of these things if you've figured out nothing is worth inspecting.\r\n\r\ni can't give too much criticism with the story. its fine, if not a little predictable at times. if you're really that interested, maybe watch someone else play it.",
